{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to propose a distributed requirement for a cross-cutting convention instead of marking each of its sites with `{impl}`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "structural: The requirement says the `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to propose a distributed requirement for a cross-cutting convention instead of marking each site with `{impl}`. The skill is a document, so the requirement's only subject is what that document says. No library, signature, type or delegation is prescribed. The text sets one obligation, which is about content.\n\nThe skill file meets it. `assets/skills/habeas-bootstrap-requirements/SKILL.md#section[Bootstrap%20requirements]/section[Proposing%20requirements]` has a paragraph that reads: \"When a convention applies on its own at many sites, such as how every validation function continues after a failed check, propose a distributed requirement instead of marking each site `{impl}`; read the habeas skill's `references/distributed-requirements.md`.\" I read this paragraph through a text search snippet, because the node reads of the file showed only elided text. The same file's Workflow section also covers authoring `sites:<full-name>` for each distributed requirement.\n\nThe supplied root is the whole skill file, and it carries the required guidance.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has one behavioral obligation. The `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill MUST direct agents to propose a distributed requirement for a cross-cutting convention, and not mark each site `{impl}`. The requirement is about the skill's text, so a text-content test is the right kind of evidence.\n\n- `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bootstrap_skill_proposes_distributed_requirements_for_conventions]` reads the bootstrap skill's `SKILL.md` and extracts the `## Proposing requirements` section with `section`. `section` panics if the heading is missing. `assert_text` then asserts that the section contains \"propose a distributed requirement instead of marking each site `{impl}`\". That phrase covers both halves of the directive. Because the check is scoped to this skill and section, text from another skill can't satisfy it.\n- `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bundled_skills_refer_to_a_topic_home_instead_of_restating_it]` also carries a `{test}` marker. It adds supporting checks that the bootstrap skill points to the `habeas` skill's `references/distributed-requirements.md`. It also checks that the skill doesn't restate other skills' home content.\n\nI found no other input class, boundary or failure case in the text. A reworded or removed directive would fail the first test, so the assertions enforce the obligation.",
